The most common two-release edit: a column every row should have.

1. Release 2 adds `slug` and fills it; it may still hold NULL, for the rows
   the old executable writes while the new one is rolled out.
2. Release 3 makes it NOT NULL, with one more step filling the rows still
   NULL (none here; written so, the step is right whatever came between).
   The rebuild then finds a slug in every row.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'Pea Soup'), (2, 'Rye Bread');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));
-- Rows written since release 2 by the old executable.
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-')) WHERE slug IS NULL;

== plan
-- after 1
roux: migrating test.db, ledger.sql's steps 1 to 2
  step 1
    ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
  step 2
    UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));
-- after 2
roux: migrating test.db, ledger.sql's step 3
  step 3
    UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-')) WHERE slug IS NULL;
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name, slug
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name", "slug") SELECT "id", "name", "slug" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"

== query
SELECT id, slug FROM dish ORDER BY id

== rows
1 | pea-soup
2 | rye-bread
